Hi Fernando,

I didn't check this extremely closely but didn't see anything that immediately stood out as an error or anything. Your priors for the hyperparameters (mu, sd's) might be a little more diffuse than I would typically employ for a logit model, but this doesn't seem to be manifesting anything clearly problematic. If I understand correctly, you also noted that results seem insensitive to changes in the hyperparameter priors, so I guess I'd leave as is. I think it's probably best not to mess around with specific prior values after fitting.

One thing I've observed with the (D)CM on occasion is that it will shrink effects "out of significance" relative to single species models in situations where one or a very small number of species may exhibit associations that are much different from the rest of the community. You could evaluate whether this is occurring by fitting single-species dynamic occupancy models (e.g., using MLE) and cross-examining estimates of colonization (or exinction/persistence, although less straight-forward). If this seems like an issue, one options might be to employ finite or non-parametric mixtures (https://doi.org/10.1002/eap.2249), or maybe a heavier-tailed distribution for the species random effects (e.g., beta[species]~dt(df[beta], mu[beta], tau[beta]). Trait effects on the hyper-means might also provide a little more flexibility.  I think each of these strategies might provide coefficients a little more freedom to vary (if this is your concern), although probably at the cost of reduced precision and extra work trying to figure out what specification makes the most sense/fits best on whole. Others may have clearer ideas.

Best,

John

