Hello again,

I've made a little progress with this, but it creates new issues, so I still can't resolve my basic problem; How can I analyse data from sites with a variable number of replicates, while still incorporating replicate-level covariates? The Dorazio/Royle model with data augmentation in AHM1 11.7 looks to include variable number of surveys and still model 'p' as a Bernoulli indexed by [i,j,k] but when I've tried this with my data, JAGS objects to the NAs I have for the missing surveys returning:

Error in checkForRemoteErrors(val) :
4 nodes produced errors; first error: RUNTIME ERROR:
Unable to resolve the following parameters:
Jul.day[80,3] (line 158)
Jul.day[81,3] (line 158)
etc.

There's no obvious difference between the AHM1 data and mine, so I can't understand why JAGS will accept the AHM example but not mine.

Inspired by AHM1 section 11.6, I could collapse 'y', the 3D array of sites x surveys x species, into 'ysum', a 2D array of sites x species, with the new values representing detection history ranging from 0 - 3 (assuming either 2 or 3 surveys per site, as per my example in the first email and the MHB analysis in AHM1). Combined with a vector 'J', which identifies the number of surveys at each site, I could then model detection history per-site as a binomial probability with J trials rather than Bernoulli. In pseudocode, the Observation model would then be:

# Observation model

        for(k in 1: nspecies){

          for (i in 1:nsite){

                          logit(p[i,k]) <- lp[k] +

                                                  alphalp1[k] * Jul.day[i]  # This line no longer correct

                      mu.p[i,k] <- z[i,k] * p[i,k]

                      ysum[i,k] ~ dbin(mu.p[i,k], J[i])

          } # / nsite
        } # / nspecies

However, then 'p' is only indexed by site and species, not survey. So I can no longer include the detection covariate 'Jul.day' as I cannot index it by survey 'j'.

If anyone is able to help with this, I would be very grateful.

Many thanks,
Tom

